1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,052
I started building software because I
had a problem I couldn't solve the way I

2
00:00:05,132 --> 00:00:05,736
wanted to.

3
00:00:05,816 --> 00:00:11,552
I was trying to sell pigs, and I couldn't
find the right way to connect with buyers

4
00:00:11,632 --> 00:00:14,288
at a price I believed they were worth.

5
00:00:14,368 --> 00:00:17,025
At the time, I wasn't
a software developer.

6
00:00:17,105 --> 00:00:20,446
I was coming from sales, customer
service, and online business.

7
00:00:20,526 --> 00:00:25,236
But that one problem eventually led me to
build Agriloop, a marketplace designed to

8
00:00:25,316 --> 00:00:26,604
connect farmers with consumers.

9
00:00:26,684 --> 00:00:30,367
And that changed the direction
I was taking with my work.

10
00:00:30,447 --> 00:00:34,131
This is not a story about building
a million-dollar company overnight.

11
00:00:34,211 --> 00:00:38,236
It's about what happens when someone from
a completely different background decides

12
00:00:38,316 --> 00:00:43,709
to learn how to build software, tries to
turn a real problem into a product, and

13
00:00:43,789 --> 00:00:48,157
discovers that getting the product
working is only one part of the job.

14
00:00:48,237 --> 00:00:51,920
I'm Andrew, and this is the
journey from sales to SaaS.

15
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,808
Before software, I spent time working in
sales, customer service, and corporate

16
00:00:55,888 --> 00:00:56,132
environments.

17
00:00:56,212 --> 00:01:00,993
One of the biggest lessons I learned was
that people aren't always buying the thing

18
00:01:01,073 --> 00:01:01,641
you're selling.

19
00:01:01,721 --> 00:01:04,233
They're buying what that
thing means to them.

20
00:01:04,313 --> 00:01:07,473
In one of my roles, I worked
around security systems.

21
00:01:07,553 --> 00:01:09,417
The customer wasn't
simply buying equipment.

22
00:01:09,497 --> 00:01:11,037
They wanted to feel safe.

23
00:01:11,117 --> 00:01:12,657
They wanted peace of mind.

24
00:01:12,737 --> 00:01:14,926
That changed how I thought about sales.

25
00:01:15,006 --> 00:01:19,462
You have to understand what matters to the
customer, what the company can actually

26
00:01:19,542 --> 00:01:23,674
deliver, and how to find a solution
that makes sense for both sides.

27
00:01:23,754 --> 00:01:27,886
I also learned that a customer's
problem can have more than one layer.

28
00:01:27,966 --> 00:01:32,747
For example, when helping people with flight
bookings, I dealt with a family of four

29
00:01:32,827 --> 00:01:34,043
travelling with eight bags.

30
00:01:34,123 --> 00:01:37,607
The baggage rules created another
problem they hadn't fully accounted for.

31
00:01:37,687 --> 00:01:42,467
I looked at the situation from their
perspective and offered an option for the extra

32
00:01:42,547 --> 00:01:42,792
baggage.

33
00:01:42,872 --> 00:01:44,412
It was a simple lesson:

34
00:01:44,492 --> 00:01:47,328
don't just respond to the
first thing someone says.

35
00:01:47,408 --> 00:01:49,920
Understand the situation they're
actually trying to solve.

36
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,576
That way of thinking followed
me into online business.

37
00:01:53,656 --> 00:01:58,451
I started building my own experience
around trading and other online ventures.

38
00:01:58,531 --> 00:02:03,733
I've spent approximately seven years
trading, although my results are not where I

39
00:02:03,812 --> 00:02:05,358
want them to be.

40
00:02:05,438 --> 00:02:08,607
This is a software project,
not trading advice.

41
00:02:08,688 --> 00:02:12,264
Trading is high risk
and you can lose money.

42
00:02:12,344 --> 00:02:18,764
My own trading results aren't where I
want them to be, and nothing here is a

43
00:02:18,844 --> 00:02:21,201
recommendation to buy or sell anything.

44
00:02:21,281 --> 00:02:25,264
I also started YardScale Digital,
working around websites, marketing, lead

45
00:02:25,344 --> 00:02:26,889
generation, and digital systems.

46
00:02:26,969 --> 00:02:32,576
I was learning how to find customers,
explain an offer, understand what a business

47
00:02:32,656 --> 00:02:36,232
needed, and turn that into
something I could deliver.

48
00:02:36,312 --> 00:02:42,732
One of the things I learned is that what a
client expects and what can realistically

49
00:02:42,812 --> 00:02:47,607
be delivered within a particular
budget are not always the same thing.

50
00:02:47,688 --> 00:02:54,107
That pushed me to think more seriously
about the kind of business I wanted to build

51
00:02:54,188 --> 00:02:54,920
long term.

52
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:56,887
Then I had an experience that
changed how I looked at software.

53
00:02:56,967 --> 00:02:58,363
I came across a client who had an app.

54
00:02:58,443 --> 00:03:00,822
That made me think differently about
what I could offer and what I could build

55
00:03:00,902 --> 00:03:00,986
myself.

56
00:03:01,066 --> 00:03:02,297
I had already been
experimenting with AI tools.

57
00:03:02,377 --> 00:03:04,756
I started using them to help with websites,
and I realised they could shorten the

58
00:03:04,836 --> 00:03:06,395
time it took to turn an
idea into something working.

59
00:03:06,475 --> 00:03:08,363
I began learning more through research,
YouTube, experimenting, and asking AI to

60
00:03:08,443 --> 00:03:09,018
explain things to me.

61
00:03:09,098 --> 00:03:10,986
I started with ChatGPT and later used
Claude heavily, especially for development.

62
00:03:11,066 --> 00:03:11,805
I wasn't an expert programmer.

63
00:03:11,885 --> 00:03:14,428
I had taught myself some coding concepts
and languages, but I still had a lot to

64
00:03:14,508 --> 00:03:14,592
learn.

65
00:03:14,672 --> 00:03:17,051
A client who already had an app helped me
see that software development could become

66
00:03:17,131 --> 00:03:18,035
part of the work I did.

67
00:03:18,115 --> 00:03:20,002
Without that experience, I might have
continued focusing mainly on agency services.

68
00:03:20,082 --> 00:03:22,625
My uncle also kept reminding me that I
was only scratching the surface of what AI

69
00:03:22,705 --> 00:03:25,084
could do, and encouraged me to become better
at explaining what I wanted from these

70
00:03:25,164 --> 00:03:25,248
tools.

71
00:03:25,328 --> 00:03:27,051
The important shift was that
I could now guide the process:

72
00:03:27,131 --> 00:03:29,018
describe the outcome, understand the
structure, review what was being created, test

73
00:03:29,098 --> 00:03:29,674
it, and keep improving.

74
00:03:29,754 --> 00:03:30,822
I still had to make the decisions.

75
00:03:30,902 --> 00:03:33,281
AI could help me move faster, but it
could not decide what customers needed or

76
00:03:33,361 --> 00:03:34,264
whether a business idea made sense.

77
00:03:34,344 --> 00:03:35,740
My first experiments were not
all serious business products.

78
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:37,871
I worked on websites, tried building a
game, and explored different app ideas.

79
00:03:37,951 --> 00:03:38,854
Some of those experiments went nowhere.

80
00:03:38,934 --> 00:03:41,313
But each one taught me something about
turning an idea into a working product, and

81
00:03:41,393 --> 00:03:43,281
about the difference between getting
something to run and making something people

82
00:03:43,361 --> 00:03:43,609
actually want.

83
00:03:43,689 --> 00:03:45,248
Over a relatively short period,
I created around 35 repositories.

84
00:03:45,328 --> 00:03:46,559
That doesn't mean I created
35 successful businesses.

85
00:03:46,639 --> 00:03:48,690
It means I was experimenting, learning,
and getting more comfortable with the tools

86
00:03:48,770 --> 00:03:49,182
and the process.

87
00:03:49,262 --> 00:03:50,986
I was also beginning to see a
different opportunity for YardScale.

88
00:03:51,066 --> 00:03:53,117
Instead of only helping businesses with
marketing and websites, I could move further

89
00:03:53,197 --> 00:03:54,920
into building software products and
working through the problems behind them.

90
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,304
Then came the problem
that led to Agriloop.

91
00:03:59,384 --> 00:04:03,139
I had been trying to sell pigs.

92
00:04:03,219 --> 00:04:10,810
I believed they were worth a certain
price, but finding the right buyer was

93
00:04:10,890 --> 00:04:11,358
difficult.

94
00:04:11,438 --> 00:04:19,578
I started looking at how people were using
social media and other online spaces to

95
00:04:19,658 --> 00:04:21,769
sell livestock and produce.

96
00:04:21,849 --> 00:04:28,893
I began thinking about a marketplace
where farmers could connect with buyers in

97
00:04:28,973 --> 00:04:32,180
their own parish and
neighbouring parishes.

98
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:33,824
That became Agriloop.

99
00:04:33,904 --> 00:04:37,112
Development started on 13 September 2026.

100
00:04:37,192 --> 00:04:39,852
The first idea was straightforward:

101
00:04:39,932 --> 00:04:48,619
give farmers a way to create listings and
give buyers a way to discover what was

102
00:04:48,699 --> 00:04:49,167
available.

103
00:04:49,247 --> 00:04:56,838
But turning that idea into software meant
learning a lot more than I expected.

104
00:04:56,918 --> 00:05:02,317
There were databases, user accounts,
listings, messaging, design decisions, bugs,

105
00:05:02,397 --> 00:05:08,345
and a lot of small details
that had to work together.

106
00:05:08,425 --> 00:05:14,920
I kept working through those challenges
until Agriloop reached a working beta.

107
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:21,062
And that's where this
journey really starts.

108
00:05:21,142 --> 00:05:33,345
Because getting an app to work is
not the same as building a business.

109
00:05:33,425 --> 00:05:44,750
You still have to find the right people,
understand whether the problem matters

110
00:05:44,830 --> 00:05:56,156
enough, earn trust, and give customers
a reason to use what you've built.

111
00:05:56,236 --> 00:06:02,297
I am still figuring those things out.

112
00:06:02,377 --> 00:06:15,458
In this series, I'm going to break down
the decisions, the tools, the mistakes, and

113
00:06:15,538 --> 00:06:24,231
the lessons behind building software
products from the ground up.

114
00:06:24,311 --> 00:06:28,618
Not just the finished screens.

115
00:06:28,698 --> 00:06:32,128
The thinking behind them.

116
00:06:32,208 --> 00:06:40,901
And the first question we
need to answer is simple:

117
00:06:40,981 --> 00:06:47,920
How do you find a problem worth solving?

